  "id": "Compound::DB06800 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Methylnaltrexone is proposed to interact with Cyp2d6 through a mechanism involving the binding of its structural analogs to the enzyme. The evidence suggests that Methylnaltrexone, which resembles Nalbuphine and Hydrocodone, both of which bind to Cyp2d6, may exert a similar effect. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds point to their shared classification as organic cyclic compounds and agents affecting the nervous system, reinforcing their potential to influence Cyp2d6 activity. The observed binding of Dihydrocodeine to Cyp2d6, which is a known substrate of the enzyme, further supports this relationship. The integrated evidence suggests that Methylnaltrexone may inhibit Cyp2d6 through a mechanism involving the interaction of its structural analogs with the enzyme, potentially leading to reduced metabolism of substrates. This mechanism is consistent with the known role of Cyp2d6 in drug metabolism and is supported by the structural similarities between Methylnaltrexone and its analogs.",
